  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Vaccines are safe, effective biological preparations that teach the immune system to recognize and defend against harmful viruses or bacteria, preventing illnesses such as influenza, COVID-19, polio, and measles. They work by using weakened, killed, or synthetic parts of germ—like mRNA—to induce immunity without causing the disease. Key Aspects of Vaccines Mechanism: Vaccines stimulate the immune system to produce antibodies, creating memory cells that protect against future infections. Types: Technologies include live-attenuated, inactivated, subunit, and mRNA vaccines, which instruct cells to make antigen proteins. Target Diseases: Essential vaccines include measles, mumps, rubella (MMR), polio, tetanus, HPV, and influenza. Safety: Vaccines are strictly tested and monitored for safety and effectiveness before and after approval. Public Health Impact: Routine immunization has significantly lowered mortality and successfully eliminated diseases like polio. Commonly Recommended Vaccinations Infants/Children: DTaP, Hepatitis B, HIB, Polio, MMRV, and HPV. Adults: Annual flu shots, COVID-19, tetanus (Tdap), and Pneumococcal. mRNA Vaccine TechnologyModern mRNA vaccines, such as those used for COVID-19, offer rapid production capabilities compared to traditional methods. These vaccines use synthetic mRNA to teach body cells how to produce antigens, prompting an immune response.",
